flash·cloud·GH#45538261205", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Anna Lühring was a soldier in the Prussian army during the Napoleonic Wars.", "newValue": "Anna Lühring (1796–1866) was a remarkable figure whose brief but impactful military service challenged 19th-century gender norms during the tumultuous final campaigns of the Napoleonic Wars. Hailing from Bremen, she disguised herself as a man to enlist in the Prussian army, participating in critical engagements that shaped the future of Europe. Her story, emblematic of the hidden roles women played in times of conflict, offers a crucial lens into the social constraints and opportunities of the Early Modern era's twilight, resonating with broader themes of national identity and individual agency.

In 1814, Lühring, then 17, adopted the male alias 'August Lühring' and joined the Lützow Free Corps, a volunteer unit renowned for its distinctive black uniforms and fierce nationalism. She served with considerable bravery, reportedly participating in the siege of Jülich and engagements in the Netherlands, though her specific involvement in the Battle of Waterloo remains a subject of historical debate. Her true identity was discovered later in 1814, leading to her honorable discharge, but not before earning recognition for her courage and dedication, including a personal commendation from Prussian King Frederick William III.

